Fewer:

P=Player, U=Unicorn, A=Altar

P=U | P=A | U=A | Result
----+-----+-----+-------------
 Y  |  Y  |  Y  | Insult to <altar>; lose wisdom; god angry
 Y  |  N  |  N  | You get converted
 N  |  Y  |  N  | Appropriately <aligned>; gain alignment; +3 value
 N  |  N  |  Y  | Insult to <altar>; lose wisdom; other god angry!
 N  |  N  |  N  | +3 value

The other options are logically impossible; if you are the same
alignment as the unicorn, and the same alignment as the altar, the
unicorn cannot be of a different alignment to the altar, for example.

I'd say that the first three of these should be reasonably obvious, even
to a beginner, as long as the player realises that unicorns have a
strict alignment at all. The only snags are that I'd expect the fourth
and possibly the fifth case to automatically convert the altar to my god
(after all, sacrificing a rival unicorn on a rival altar should be
rather pleasing to a god, especially if the two belong together), but in
fact both can (the fourth not improbably) have negative effects.

